## Proof

Let the ellipsoid be $E = \{x \in \mathbb{R}^n : \sum_{i=1}^n x_i^2/a_i^2 \leq 1\}$ with $a_1 \geq a_2 \geq \cdots \geq a_n > 0$, centered at the origin. A central hyperplane section is $E \cap H$ where $H = \{x : u \cdot x = 0\}$ for a unit vector $u = (u_1, \ldots, u_n)$. Its "surface area" is its $(n-1)$-dimensional volume.

**Computing the section volume.** The ellipsoid $E$ is the image of the unit ball $B_n$ under the linear map $A = \operatorname{diag}(a_1, \ldots, a_n)$. We have
$$E \cap H = A\bigl(B_n \cap \{y : (A^T u) \cdot y = 0\}\bigr).$$
Set $v = A^T u = (a_1 u_1, \ldots, a_n u_n)$. The set $B_n \cap v^\perp$ is the unit $(n-1)$-ball lying in the hyperplane $v^\perp$, with $(n-1)$-volume $\omega_{n-1}$.

The map $A$ sends $v^\perp$ onto $H$ (since $u \cdot (Ay) = (A^T u)\cdot y = v \cdot y = 0$ for $y \in v^\perp$). The $(n-1)$-dimensional volume scaling factor of $A$ restricted to $v^\perp$ is, by the standard formula for the Jacobian of a linear map on a co-dimension-one subspace,
$$|\det A|\cdot\|A^{-T}\hat v\|,\qquad \hat v = v/\|v\|.$$
Since $A^{-T}v = A^{-T}A^T u = u$ and $\|u\|=1$, this becomes
$$\frac{|\det A|}{\|v\|} = \frac{\prod_{i=1}^n a_i}{\|A^T u\|} = \frac{\prod_{i=1}^n a_i}{\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2 u_i^2}}.$$
Therefore
$$\operatorname{Vol}_{n-1}(E \cap H) \;=\; \omega_{n-1}\,\frac{\prod_{i=1}^n a_i}{\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2 u_i^2}}.$$

**Maximizing.** To maximize the section volume over all unit vectors $u$, we minimize the denominator $\sqrt{\sum a_i^2 u_i^2}$ subject to $\sum u_i^2 = 1$. Since $a_n \le a_i$ for every $i$,
$$\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2 u_i^2 \;\ge\; a_n^2 \sum_{i=1}^n u_i^2 \;=\; a_n^2,$$
with equality if and only if $u = \pm e_n$. Hence the maximum section volume is
$$\omega_{n-1}\,\frac{\prod_{i=1}^n a_i}{a_n} \;=\; \omega_{n-1}\prod_{i=1}^{n-1} a_i,$$
achieved uniquely (up to sign of the normal) by $u = e_n$, i.e., by the hyperplane $x_n = 0$.

**Conclusion.** The central hyperplane section of largest surface area is the one **perpendicular to the shortest semi-axis** of the ellipsoid — equivalently, the section spanned by the $n-1$ longest semi-axes. It is the $(n-1)$-dimensional ellipsoid with semi-axes $a_1,\ldots,a_{n-1}$.

